{"text": "FROM: Frank Saunders, Saul Warshaw\n\nPHILIP MORRIS. INCORPORATED -  RUDER & FINN INCORPORATED\n100 Park Avenue 110 East 59th Street\n: \u201cNew York, New York 10017 New York, New York 10022\n- (212) 679-1800 (212) 593-6355\n\nFOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE\n\nTWELVE YEAR SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH LEADS\nTO MAJOR BREAKTHROUGH AT PHILIP MORRIS\n\nHigh Flavor - 9 mg. Tar MERIT To Be Introduced Nationally.\n\nNEW YORK, N.Y., December 16 -~ The product of twelve years of research\ninto the flavor components of tobacco smoke--MERIT, a new high flavor yet\nlow tar cigarette--was announced at a press conference today by Joseph F.\nCullman 3rd, chairman and chief executive officer of Philip Morris\nIncorporated.\nResearch culminating with the production of 9 mg. MERIT, according\nto Dr. Helmut R. R. Wakeham, vice president, science and technology, was\napproached on three fronts--modification of blend, flavor\u2019 amplification and\nthe use of modern filtration technology.\n\u201cThe most significant breakthrough in the development of MERIT came\nwith the discovery of what has been labeled \u2018Enriched Flavor,\u2019 \" said\nOr. Wakeham. Essentially, he explained, this term describes the result\nof a process that isolates high intensity taste constituents in smoke\n\nand re-introduces them back into the blend.\n\nSTOSSstzooT\n\nTechnical instrumentation, the gas chromatograph (GC), and the\n\nmass spectrometer played major roles in MERIT's evolution. As described\n\n-more-",
